# CI-OS Bazzite (Phase 5) — image DEFINITION only. NOT an ISO/installer
# pipeline (explicitly out of scope for this pass — Bazzite's own upstream
# project already provides ISO/install tooling for its base images; see
# docs/architecture/multi-distro-design.md).
#
# ============================================================================
# WHY THIS BACKEND LOOKS NOTHING LIKE backends/{ubuntu,mint,fedora,arch}/
# ============================================================================
# Every prior backend writes a script that runs ON an already-installed,
# already-running system and imperatively installs packages / edits config
# files in place. Bazzite is Fedora Atomic (bootc/rpm-ostree): the OS root
# (/usr, and /etc's *defaults*) is built here, as an OCI image, and later
# `bootc switch`ed onto hardware or booted as a VM/ISO. /usr is READ-ONLY at
# runtime; /etc is a special overlay that DOES allow local admin edits;
# /var (and therefore /home, a symlink into /var/home) is EXCLUDED from the
# image commit entirely — it's machine-local state seeded at first boot.
# That last fact is the one that shapes almost every RUN step below: see
# core/manifest/role-map/bootc.yaml's header for the full "why $HOME can't
# be baked" reasoning (uv/homebrew/asdf, and — a genuine finding of this
# pass, not assumed — even `ci_user`'s home directory).
#
# Base image: per research (see docs/architecture/multi-distro-design.md,
# Phase 5), ublue-os's own canonical starter (ublue-os/image-template) FROMs
# a PINNED `ghcr.io/ublue-os/bazzite:stable@sha256:...` for a *custom*
# derivative image. Pinned below to the digest `:stable` actually resolved
# to when this file was verified with `docker build --check` /
# `docker manifest inspect` on 2026-09-17 (~4.7 GiB, linux/amd64-only, 128
# layers) — NOT a guess: this is a real digest this environment's Docker
# Desktop (a genuine Linux VM, network-connected) confirmed by pulling it.
# `:stable` is a rolling tag on ublue-os's side, so this digest WILL go
# stale — re-resolve it (`docker manifest inspect ghcr.io/ublue-os/bazzite:stable`,
# or `skopeo inspect docker://ghcr.io/ublue-os/bazzite:stable`) on a normal
# cadence rather than treating this pin as permanent.
#
# DESKTOP TARGET: KDE Plasma, specifically the Mesa (non-NVIDIA) desktop tag.
# Per research: Bazzite ships 10 image variants across two DE families (KDE
# from Kinoite, GNOME from Silverblue); KDE is unambiguously the flagship —
# it's the unqualified `bazzite` tag, listed first in the project's own
# variant matrix, and is what "Bazzite" means by default in its own docs and
# community (GNOME is the explicitly-named alternate). This deliberately
# breaks the GNOME pattern every prior backend followed — see
# core/manifest/role-map/bootc.yaml and this file's KDE section below for
# what that means for theming.
FROM ghcr.io/ublue-os/bazzite:stable@sha256:ccdba12ff88bcff33c80d90200ed7e3b205373d22ec79319287d4e4fcda75fae

# ============================================================================
# HOW TOKEN-DERIVED ARTIFACTS GET INTO THIS IMAGE — build-time approach and
# why, since a Containerfile build context normally needs pre-rendered files
# already on disk before `podman build` runs (every prior backend renders
# directly onto a LIVE target system; nothing in this repo has needed a
# render-before-build step until now):
#
#   (1) Pure raster renders with no dependency on anything THIS image
#       installs (the GRUB background PNG, the Plymouth watermark PNG — both
#       just a fixed logo composited onto a token-colored canvas) are
#       rendered on the HOST, once, by build.just's `render` recipe, into
#       backends/bazzite/image/staging/, and COPY'd in below. This is the
#       literal shape this task's brief suggested, and it's the right one
#       here: it needs no extra base image, no extra RUN layer, and reuses
#       core/render/render-grub-background.sh completely unmodified.
#
#   (2) core/render/render-motd.sh is ALSO run as a real build-time RUN step
#       (not staged) — it's plain bash with no external tool dependency
#       beyond `require_root` (true inside any RUN step) and it writes
#       directly into a fixed absolute path (/etc/update-motd.d), so
#       "pre-render to a staging dir and COPY" would just mean COPYing that
#       same directory back to itself. Genuinely viable to invoke unmodified
#       inside this build, so it is.
#
#   (3) Plymouth's spinner.plymouth/bgrt.plymouth text configs are the one
#       artifact that structurally CANNOT be pre-staged on the host: those
#       files ship inside the plymouth-theme-spinner/plymouth-theme-bgrt
#       RPMs and don't exist anywhere until dnf5 installs them — which only
#       happens inside this build. So those are patched with a RUN step
#       AFTER the packages are installed, sourcing vendor/brand/tokens/
#       tokens.sh for the hex values, replicating (not copy-pasting — the
#       output-path/backup-dir plumbing differs) the exact 4-key sed
#       transform backends/ubuntu/desktop-theme/10-plymouth.sh applies, for
#       the same reason a dedicated core/render/render-plymouth-theme.sh
#       script was NOT added for this: it would have exactly one caller,
#       operating on files this same RUN step already has open, and adding
#       a new core/render/ script was out of this task's file list.
#
# The upshot: no separate "renderer" build stage was needed after all —
# every artifact either renders cleanly on the host (staged + COPY'd) or
# renders cleanly against files already present in THIS stage once the
# right packages are installed (a RUN step, in place). A two-stage build
# (a throwaway Fedora stage just to pre-stage the Plymouth sed output) was
# considered and rejected: it would need to install the exact same
# plymouth-theme-* packages a second time in a second, unrelated base image
# and trust their content matches what Bazzite's own Fedora package set
# ships — strictly worse than patching the real thing in place, once.
# ============================================================================

# --- Ensure the Plymouth theme packages this build patches are present ----
# (Bazzite's base almost certainly ships at least one of these already —
# Plymouth is required for any bootc/Atomic boot splash — but installing
# unconditionally makes the later sed step's file-existence checks a
# statement of fact instead of a guess.)
RUN dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
      plymouth plymouth-theme-spinner plymouth-theme-bgrt \
    && dnf5 clean all

# --- Token-derived Plymouth colors — see reasoning block (3) above --------
# Same 4 keys, same source-of-truth tokens, as every other backend's
# 10-plymouth.sh step; replicated here (not sourced/exec'd) because that
# script also does interactive-upgrade bookkeeping (a timestamped backup
# dir, update-alternatives calls, update-initramfs) that makes no sense for
# a from-scratch image build, and because backends/ubuntu/ content is out of
# scope to edit for this task.
COPY vendor/brand/tokens/tokens.sh /tmp/ci-tokens.sh
RUN . /tmp/ci-tokens.sh && \
    BG="$(ci_hex0x "$CI_DARK_BACKGROUND")" && \
    PBG="$(ci_hex0x "$CI_DARK_SECONDARY")" && \
    PFG="$(ci_hex0x "$CI_DARK_PRIMARY")" && \
    for f in /usr/share/plymouth/themes/spinner/spinner.plymouth /usr/share/plymouth/themes/bgrt/bgrt.plymouth; do \
      if [ -f "$f" ]; then \
        sed -i "s/BackgroundStartColor=0x[0-9a-fA-F]*/BackgroundStartColor=$BG/" "$f"; \
        sed -i "s/BackgroundEndColor=0x[0-9a-fA-F]*/BackgroundEndColor=$BG/" "$f"; \
        sed -i "s/ProgressBarBackgroundColor=0x[0-9a-fA-F]*/ProgressBarBackgroundColor=$PBG/" "$f"; \
        sed -i "s/ProgressBarForegroundColor=0x[0-9a-fA-F]*/ProgressBarForegroundColor=$PFG/" "$f"; \
      fi; \
    done
COPY backends/bazzite/image/staging/plymouth-watermark.png /usr/share/plymouth/themes/spinner/watermark.png

# --- Activate the theme + regenerate the initramfs that embeds it ---------
# BEST-EFFORT / UNVERIFIED, disclosed rather than silently trusted (see
# docs/architecture/multi-distro-design.md's Phase 5 research and this
# backend's own implementation report): on Fedora Atomic, bootc/rpm-ostree
# normally owns the kernel+initramfs pairing and copies it to /boot at
# DEPLOY time, not build time — so it's genuinely unclear how much of this
# even needs to happen here vs. gets regenerated for free when the image is
# deployed. `plymouth-set-default-theme -R` (the -R = regenerate-initramfs
# flag) is attempted first since it's the one-command idiomatic path; a
# plain `dracut -f` (what Ubuntu's 10-plymouth.sh runs via
# update-initramfs) is deliberately NOT used as a fallback — research found
# that a hostonly dracut run inside a container build (no matching
# kernel/hardware context) is a known failure mode; `--no-hostonly` is the
# documented-safer form the coreOS layering examples use, so that is the
# fallback instead. Both are non-fatal (`|| true`) — a failure here should
# not sink the whole image build, since this is best-effort activation, not
# baseline correctness (the theme FILES are correctly in place either way).
RUN plymouth-set-default-theme -R bgrt 2>/dev/null \
    || (plymouth-set-default-theme bgrt 2>/dev/null && dracut --no-hostonly -f 2>/dev/null) \
    || true

# --- GRUB: background + branding-only keys, NEVER a config regen ----------
# Research: Fedora Atomic desktops (F41+, which covers current Bazzite) use
# a STATIC, pre-supplied GRUB2 config with BLS entries, not the
# grub2-mkconfig/update-grub dynamic-regeneration model every prior
# backend's 15-grub-theme.sh assumes — running grub2-mkconfig on a live
# Atomic system is reported broken (composefs-related errors) as of this
# research. So: set the same branding-only keys Ubuntu's script sets
# (core/lib/grub-merge.sh's ci_grub_merge_key is a pure idempotent
# sed/grep key-setter with no mkconfig call inside it — safe to reuse
# unmodified), but do NOT call grub2-mkconfig/update-grub/grub-mkconfig
# anywhere in this file. Unlike Ubuntu's script (which treats a missing
# /etc/default/grub as a hard failure), this creates the file if absent —
# whether Bazzite's static BLS config actually reads these keys the same
# way was NOT independently verified against a live bootc box, so failing
# the whole image build over it would be the wrong trade-off; the PNG is
# copied and the keys are set either way (harmless if unused), same
# disclosed-gap shape as backends/fedora's MOTD note.
COPY backends/bazzite/image/staging/ci-os-grub-bg.png /usr/share/pixmaps/ci-os-grub-bg.png
COPY core/lib/grub-merge.sh /tmp/grub-merge.sh
RUN [ -f /etc/default/grub ] || touch /etc/default/grub && \
    . /tmp/grub-merge.sh && \
    ci_grub_merge_key /etc/default/grub GRUB_BACKGROUND "/usr/share/pixmaps/ci-os-grub-bg.png" && \
    ci_grub_merge_key /etc/default/grub GRUB_DISTRIBUTOR "Companion OS" && \
    ci_grub_merge_key /etc/default/grub GRUB_GFXMODE "auto" && \
    ci_grub_merge_key /etc/default/grub GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE "menu" && \
    ci_grub_merge_key /etc/default/grub GRUB_TIMEOUT "5"

# --- MOTD — see reasoning block (2) above: real, unmodified script run ----
# UNVERIFIED (same honesty convention carried over from backends/fedora and
# backends/arch, neither of which could confirm this on a live box either):
# whether /etc/update-motd.d + PAM's pam_motd actually wires into login on
# Fedora Atomic at all. The files are written either way (harmless if
# unused).
COPY core/render/render-motd.sh /tmp/ci-render/core/render/render-motd.sh
COPY core/lib/theme-lib.sh /tmp/ci-render/core/lib/theme-lib.sh
COPY vendor/brand/tokens/tokens.sh /tmp/ci-render/vendor/brand/tokens/tokens.sh
RUN mkdir -p /etc/update-motd.d && \
    REPO_ROOT=/tmp/ci-render bash /tmp/ci-render/core/render/render-motd.sh

# --- KDE Plasma color scheme — see core/manifest/role-map/bootc.yaml's ----
# desktop-target note. backends/_mixins/kde-plasma/{65-kde-theme.sh,
# CI-OS-Dark.colors} existed but were never exercised by any backend before
# this one (Ubuntu/Mint/Fedora/Arch are all GNOME). Per research: this
# backend is the first real target for that mixin's DATA, but not its
# SCRIPT — 65-kde-theme.sh is written for a LIVE session (it shells out to
# kwriteconfig5 + qdbus against a running Plasma Shell), which has no
# meaning during an image build; baking the default a different, simpler
# way makes that script's live-apply path unnecessary for the *default*
# color scheme specifically. KDE reads kdeglobals from $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
# (/etc/xdg) as a fallback whenever a user's own ~/.config/kdeglobals
# doesn't set a scheme — so COPYing the color scheme file (unmodified) plus
# a small, new (not modifying backends/_mixins/ content) /etc/xdg/kdeglobals
# bakes the system-wide default with no first-boot step and no live-session
# tool at all.
#
# KNOWN BUG FOUND WHILE READING THE MIXIN (reported per this task's brief,
# NOT fixed — out of scope, and backends/_mixins/ content must not be
# edited): backends/_mixins/kde-plasma/65-kde-theme.sh detects and calls
# `kwriteconfig5`, but Plasma 6 (what Bazzite ships) renamed that tool to
# `kwriteconfig6` — minimal Plasma 6 installs don't carry a kwriteconfig5
# shim. That script's live-session apply path will silently fall through to
# its own "install manually" warning on Bazzite as written. Irrelevant to
# THIS file (which never invokes that script), but relevant to anyone who
# runs it by hand afterward to retroactively fix an already-provisioned
# user/session.
#
# core/render/render-kde-colorscheme.sh was deliberately NOT written for
# this pass — see this backend's implementation report for the full
# reasoning (CI-OS-Dark.colors's own header already documents it as
# hand-derived from the exact tokens.sh values, and its ~15 semantically
# distinct color roles aren't a mechanical token substitution the way the
# GRUB PNG is; a real yak-shave, not a clean drop-in for this phase).
COPY backends/_mixins/kde-plasma/CI-OS-Dark.colors /usr/share/color-schemes/CI-OS-Dark.colors
COPY backends/bazzite/image/files/kdeglobals /etc/xdg/kdeglobals

# --- NO FONT CHANGES ANYWHERE (this whole rebuild effort's own rule) ------
# Manrope stays limited to Plymouth/terminal/MOTD/setup-portal, as it already
# is on every other backend; nothing in this file installs, copies, or
# references a font.

# ============================================================================
# ECOSYSTEM — core/manifest/role-map/bootc.yaml documents the full
# baked-vs-first-boot breakdown this section implements; read it alongside
# this section, not instead of it.
# ============================================================================

# --- Docker vs. Podman (role-map: `docker`) — adapt to Podman -------------
# Bazzite ships Podman + Distrobox by default; this backend deliberately
# does NOT layer Docker CE (see the role-map entry for the full research-
# backed reasoning). Enabling the Docker-API-compatible socket and
# installing podman-compose are both safe, deterministic build-time steps —
# neither depends on runtime/hardware state.
RUN systemctl enable podman.socket && \
    dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False podman-compose && \
    dnf5 clean all

# --- CONFIRMED, plain dnf5-installable tools (role-map: gh, cloudflared,
# chromium) — same repos/packages backends/fedora/provision/provision.sh
# uses, since Bazzite shares Fedora's RPM/repo universe. -------------------
# dnf5-plugins provides the config-manager subcommand every addrepo call
# below needs (mirrors provision.sh's ensure_config_manager_plugin() —
# installed defensively rather than assumed present, even though a full
# desktop image like Bazzite's very likely already carries it).
RUN dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False dnf5-plugins && \
    dnf5 -y config-manager addrepo --from-repofile="https://cli.github.com/packages/rpm/gh-cli.repo" && \
    dnf5 -y config-manager addrepo --from-repofile="https://pkg.cloudflare.com/cloudflared.repo" && \
    dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False gh cloudflared chromium && \
    dnf5 clean all

# --- VS Code (role-map: vscode) — Microsoft's yum repo ---------------------
# CONFIRMED via research to work identically to Fedora's dnf.yaml entry.
# Unlike provision.sh's install_vscode(), there is no direct-.rpm fallback
# here on purpose: a build-time failure should fail the image build loudly,
# not silently degrade the way an already-running system's provisioning
# continues past one failed step.
RUN rpm --import https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc && \
    printf '%s\n' \
      '[code]' \
      'name=Visual Studio Code' \
      'baseurl=https://packages.microsoft.com/yumrepos/vscode' \
      'enabled=1' \
      'gpgcheck=1' \
      'gpgkey=https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc' \
      > /etc/yum.repos.d/vscode.repo && \
    dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False code && \
    dnf5 clean all

# --- Tailscale (role-map: tailscale) — VERSION_ID resolved AT BUILD TIME --
# Mirrors provision.sh's install_tailscale() exactly rather than hardcoding
# the Fedora version Bazzite happens to track today (44), so this doesn't
# silently go stale on the next Bazzite rebase.
RUN . /etc/os-release && \
    dnf5 -y config-manager addrepo --from-repofile="https://pkgs.tailscale.com/stable/fedora/${VERSION_ID}/tailscale.repo" && \
    dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False tailscale && \
    dnf5 clean all

# --- asdf/Node.js + uv build-time prerequisites (role-map: asdf, node, ----
# uv) — the EXECUTION of these three (git clone into $HOME, curl-pipe
# install into $HOME) genuinely cannot be baked (see role-map's uv/asdf/node
# entries and backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.sh's own
# header), but their BUILD toolchain — git, curl, gnupg2, and a compiler
# (gcc/gcc-c++/make, asdf-nodejs's fallback path when no prebuilt binary
# matches this architecture) — is exactly the kind of /usr-only content
# every other RUN step in this file already bakes. Ported from
# backends/fedora/provision/provision.sh's install_asdf_nodejs_dnf_prereqs()
# (BEST-EFFORT translation there too — Fedora's own header already flags
# this exact package list as not independently re-verified by that phase's
# research; reused here unchanged, not re-guessed). git/curl are very likely
# already present on a KDE desktop image; installed unconditionally anyway,
# same "state a fact, not a guess" reasoning the Plymouth RUN step above
# uses.
RUN dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
      git curl gnupg2 gcc gcc-c++ make \
    && dnf5 clean all

# --- LACT (role-map: lact) — a genuine improvement over Fedora's/Arch's ---
# `unresolved`/pacman.yaml comparison: LACT publishes a real Fedora COPR,
# independently confirmed by this backend's own research (not inherited
# from Fedora's stub, which never checked COPR). lactd.service does not
# auto-enable on install (COPR RPMs follow the same convention docker-ce's
# RPM and Arch's pacman packages do) — enabled explicitly.
RUN dnf5 -y copr enable ilyaz/lact && \
    dnf5 install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False lact && \
    systemctl enable lactd.service && \
    dnf5 clean all

# --- Ollama (role-map: ollama) — binary + systemd unit only ---------------
# CONFIRMED package-manager-agnostic (writes only to /usr + /etc, never
# $HOME) — safe to bake, unlike uv/homebrew/asdf. `systemctl enable` is
# baked; the GPU-specific drop-in (needs /dev/kfd, real hardware) and the
# model pull (the plan's own explicit first-boot decision, reconfirmed by
# research) are NOT here — see role-map's ollama_gpu_dropin/ollama_models
# entries; both belong to the sibling first-boot module.
RUN curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh && \
    systemctl enable ollama.service

# --- Companion Launcher (role-map: companion_launcher) — INTENTIONALLY ----
# STUBBED, same class of gap as backends/fedora's/backends/arch's: no
# Fedora/.rpm build is published anywhere for Companion Launcher —
# dl.ci.computer only ships a .deb — and Bazzite's dnf5-based build tooling
# doesn't change that. Deliberately not attempted; there is nothing to RUN
# here, so no RUN step exists for it (a no-op with a log message, the
# pattern every other backend's provision.sh uses, has no equivalent
# concept for something that literally does not run — the absence itself
# IS the documentation, backed by the role-map entry above).

# --- First-boot module wiring — closing the loop with backends/bazzite/ ---
# modules/, NOT authored by this file. Four first-boot units are staged and
# enabled here:
#   - ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub / ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models — CI-Hub
#     deploy and Ollama model pull, both needing registry credentials/
#     device pairing/multi-GB downloads that genuinely cannot be baked.
#   - ci-os-first-boot-core-tools — the `ci` system user, uv, and asdf/Node,
#     all of which write into $HOME (/var/home on ostree, excluded from the
#     image commit) so cannot be baked either — see
#     ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.sh's own header for the full reasoning,
#     including why this file's asdf/Node/uv BUILD toolchain (git, curl,
#     gnupg2, gcc, gcc-c++, make — installed in the RUN step above) is baked
#     here even though the actual asdf/Node/uv installs are not.
#   - ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate — a conditional-enablement decision
#     for core/headless/payload/'s captive-portal units. Those units
#     themselves are NOT baked into this image at all (see the "NOT IN THIS
#     FILE" block below) — the gate script's own presence-check degrades to
#     a harmless no-op when it finds none of them, so staging/enabling this
#     one unit is forward-compatible with a later pass adding that
#     subsystem, not a currently-functional feature.
# core/lib/*.sh (ALL of it, not just ci-hub.sh — ci-hub.sh's own
# SCRIPT_DIR-relative `bash "${SCRIPT_DIR}/generate-tunnel-certs.sh"` call
# needs its sibling present) is staged at /usr/lib/ci-os/bootstrap/core/lib/,
# the EXACT convention backends/ubuntu/chroot/customize-chroot.sh already
# uses for its own first-boot bootstrap staging — reused, not invented.
# `systemctl enable` against an image root with no running systemd is valid
# here (creates the multi-user.target.wants/ symlink; see
# ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.service's own header for why this works without a
# booted system, the same principle Ubuntu's chroot script uses `ln -sf` for
# by hand).
COPY core/lib/ /usr/lib/ci-os/bootstrap/core/lib/
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.sh /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.sh
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models.sh /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models.sh
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.sh /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.sh
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate.sh /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate.sh
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/lib/ /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/lib/
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.service
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models.service
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.service
COPY backends/bazzite/modules/ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate.service
RUN chmod +x /usr/lib/ci-os/bootstrap/core/lib/*.sh \
             /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/*.sh \
             /usr/lib/ci-os/modules/lib/*.sh && \
    systemctl enable \
      ci-os-first-boot-ci-hub.service \
      ci-os-first-boot-ollama-models.service \
      ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.service \
      ci-os-first-boot-setup-portal-gate.service

# --- Cleanup: build-only render inputs never belong in the shipped image --
RUN rm -f /tmp/ci-tokens.sh /tmp/grub-merge.sh && rm -rf /tmp/ci-render

# ============================================================================
# NOT IN THIS FILE, ON PURPOSE (see core/manifest/role-map/bootc.yaml):
#   - The ACTUAL RUNNING of: core/lib/ci-hub.sh's deploy, Ollama's model
#     pull, and uv/asdf/Node/`ci`-user setup. All four are staged and
#     enabled here (see "First-boot module wiring" above) — what's deferred
#     is EXECUTION, not wiring, because each needs something that genuinely
#     cannot exist at image-build time: ci-hub needs registry credentials/
#     device pairing (secrets never belong baked into a publicly-
#     distributed image layer, same "gated by design" reasoning
#     ecosystem.yaml already states for every backend); Ollama's model pull
#     needs network + is a deliberate multi-GB-bloat-avoidance decision;
#     uv/asdf/Node/`ci`-user all write into $HOME (/var/home on ostree,
#     structurally excluded from the image commit).
#   - Homebrew — NOT staged/wired at all (unlike the four above), because
#     it's opt-in/manual on every other backend too (tier: extended, never
#     part of the default first-boot flow) — leaving it out here doesn't
#     regress anything a fresh appliance would otherwise have gotten by
#     default. See ci-os-first-boot-core-tools.sh's header.
#   - core/headless/payload/'s captive-portal subsystem (hostapd/dnsmasq/
#     nginx/Flask setup-backend + static portal) — see the "First-boot
#     module wiring" section above for why this is a disclosed scope
#     boundary, not an oversight.
#   - ROCm (unresolved — see role-map; same Fedora-repo gap as
#     backends/fedora, partially offset by Bazzite's own better baseline)
# All of the above are either first-boot EXECUTION (this file stages and
